## How to Know When Your Nails Are Ready for Polish or Enhancements Again

Check for rigidity, not appearance. A recovered nail holds its shape when you press lightly on the tip instead of folding, and that structural test matters more than how the nail looks under polish.

Run through this before booking your next gel or acrylic appointment. The nail should feel firm across its full length, not just at the base, with no lifting at the free edge and no persistent soreness underneath. Length should have grown back on its own, since a nail that still needs enhancement to hide thinness is not ready for one. And the surface should look consistent, without the visible line between old, thin nail and new growth that marked the earlier weeks.

Most people hit this point around the three to four month mark, occasionally sooner with consistent care. If you are still seeing any bend at the tip, give it another few weeks before reapplying anything. The recovered plate you protect now is the one that will hold up under enhancements again later.

